## Proof

**Setup.** The group $K$ is generated by $(\mathbb{R}_{>0})^n$ (positive diagonal matrices) and $S_n$ (permutation matrices). Conjugating a diagonal matrix by a permutation matrix permutes its diagonal entries, so $K = (\mathbb{R}_{>0})^n \rtimes S_n$ — the group of **monomial matrices with positive entries** (exactly one positive entry per row and column, all other entries zero).

Every element of $K$ already has non-negative entries. The substantive question is whether $K$ is the **maximal** subgroup of $\mathrm{GL}_n(\mathbb{R})$ with this property — that is, whether every subgroup $H \leq \mathrm{GL}_n(\mathbb{R})$ consisting of non-negative matrices must lie inside $K$.

---

**Theorem.** *For $n \geq 1$, the group $K$ of monomial matrices with positive entries is the unique maximal subgroup of $\mathrm{GL}_n(\mathbb{R})$ whose elements all have non-negative entries. Equivalently, every subgroup $H$ of $\mathrm{GL}_n(\mathbb{R})$ in which every element has non-negative entries satisfies $H \leq K$.*

**Proof.** The forward inclusion ($K$ has non-negative entries) is immediate since every element of $K$ is monomial with positive entries.

For the converse, let $H \leq \mathrm{GL}_n(\mathbb{R})$ with every element having non-negative entries. We show every $A \in H$ lies in $K$.

Since $H$ is a group, $A \in H$ implies $A^{-1} \in H$, so **both** $A$ and $B := A^{-1}$ have non-negative entries. Write $A = (a_{ij})$, $B = (b_{ij})$ with $a_{ij} \geq 0$, $b_{ij} \geq 0$.

**Step 1: Orthogonality constraints.** From $AB = I_n$: for $i \neq j$,
$$\sum_{k=1}^n a_{ik}\, b_{kj} = 0.$$
Every term is non-negative, so $a_{ik}\, b_{kj} = 0$ for all $k$. Similarly, from $BA = I_n$: for $i \neq j$,
$$b_{ik}\, a_{kj} = 0 \quad \text{for all } k.$$

**Step 2: Each row of $A$ has exactly one nonzero entry.** Since $A$ is invertible, every row has at least one nonzero entry. Let $S_i = \{k : a_{ik} > 0\}$ be the support of row $i$.

Suppose for contradiction that $|S_i| \geq 2$ for some row $i$. Pick distinct $k_1, k_2 \in S_i$. Since $a_{i k_1} > 0$ and $a_{i k_1} b_{k_1 j} = 0$ for all $j \neq i$, we get $b_{k_1 j} = 0$ for all $j \neq i$. Likewise $b_{k_2 j} = 0$ for all $j \neq i$.

Thus rows $k_1$ and $k_2$ of $B$ are both supported only in column $i$: row $k_1 = b_{k_1 i}\, e_i^\top$ and row $k_2 = b_{k_2 i}\, e_i^\top$. These two rows are scalar multiples of $e_i^\top$, hence linearly dependent. This contradicts the invertibility of $B$. $\quad\Rightarrow\!\Leftarrow$

Therefore $|S_i| = 1$ for every $i$: **each row of $A$ has exactly one nonzero entry.**

**Step 3: $A$ is monomial with positive entries.** Since $A$ is invertible and each row has exactly one nonzero entry, no two rows can share the same nonzero column (otherwise two rows would be proportional, contradicting invertibility). By the pigeonhole principle on $n$ rows and $n$ columns, each column also has exactly one nonzero entry. So $A$ is a **monomial matrix**. Since $A$ has non-negative entries, its nonzero entries are strictly positive. Therefore $A \in K$.

Since $A \in H$ was arbitrary, $H \subseteq K$, i.e., $H \leq K$. $\blacksquare$

---

**Conclusion.** The group $K$ **is** the unique maximal subgroup of $\mathrm{GL}_n(\mathbb{R})$ consisting of matrices with non-negative entries. Every subgroup $H$ of $\mathrm{GL}_n(\mathbb{R})$ whose elements all have non-negative entries is automatically a subgroup of $K$, and conversely every subgroup of $K$ has this property. The key insight is that in any group of non-negative invertible matrices, both a matrix and its inverse are non-negative, which forces each matrix to be monomial with positive entries.

The condition $n \geq 3$ ensures $K$ has rich internal structure (since $S_n$ is non-abelian for $n \geq 3$, $K$ contains genuinely non-abelian non-negative matrix subgroups), but the maximality result holds for all $n \geq 1$.

